**Definition 3.5 (immutable target binding).** The binding $b$ is the exact
tuple of format/layout identifier, $H,\xi$, original initialization and
acquired seed-origin references, original/installed version and activation
references, cover/package creation references, target $U(k)$ and the
target's actual version/raw-table phase. Code/layout equality is literal
description equality relative to the fixed serializers. Origin equality
is exact descriptor/creation-chain equality, not a hash-collision premise.
The binding contains no seed contents, ingress reservoir, raw state or
replay-key sidecar. An activation reference identifies its actual record;
it does not provide a missing consumed input word.

Partition these fields into target fields and later-service fields. The
target's source/preparation, ordinal and actual version/phase are checked
against the decoded historical prefix. The later service, activation,
cover/package and publication-origin chain is checked against the current
immutable registry/installation records. A pre-Install target does not
contain the later activation or package creation. Those later references
must not be required to occur in its decoded prefix, nor used as an extra
old variable-input reservoir. If the target is after activation, its own
extracted activation must additionally agree with that service chain.

The published view word is the exact five-field tuple

$$
\operatorname{ViewRef}(q,b,d,v)=[\mathsf{ViewRef},U(q),b,d,v],
\qquad d\in\{00,01,10,11\}=\{S,B,T,M\}.
\tag{PVC.5}
$$

Here $v$ is exactly one body, framed once by the tuple grammar. The format
tag is the fixed word 00110110. Other new negative result tags are the
fixed words 00110111, 00111000, 00111001, 00111010, 00111011 for NoImage,
NoView, InvalidView, UnsupportedVersion, and RegistryFault respectively.
Their fields are the request identifiers and typed fault code, in that
order. They inhabit the new result parser; original RCA result arms retain
their original codec. The selecting registry arm fixes which parser is used:
an image entry uses the original Image parser and a view entry the new
ViewRef parser. No global disjointness of these literal words from an
unspecified old result-tag encoding is assumed. Exact tags, arity and
field exhaustion are required inside the selected parser.

